# libseccomp -- the syscall filter behind flatpak's sandbox.
#
# flatpak's seccomp option defaults to ENABLED, and it should stay that way: a
# sandbox without a syscall filter that still calls itself a sandbox is worse
# than no sandbox, because everything downstream reasons about it as if the
# filter were there. So this is a required package rather than an optional
# hardening step.
#
# The kernel side is already in place -- CONFIG_SECCOMP and
# CONFIG_SECCOMP_FILTER are both set in pkgs/linux/config/common.config -- so
# this is the userspace half of a capability the kernel already has.
#
# Not an LFS package, so it is pinned here rather than in versions.env.
SRC_URL=https://github.com/seccomp/libseccomp/releases/download/v2.6.0/libseccomp-2.6.0.tar.gz
SRC_SHA256=83b6085232d1588c379dc9b9cae47bb37407cf262e6e74993c61ba72d2a784dc
SRC_DIR=libseccomp-2.6.0

# --disable-static IS LOAD-BEARING HERE, AND IT IS THE FIRST TIME IN THIS
# WORKSTREAM THAT IT HAS BEEN. That is worth stating because six recipes in the
# GnuPG chain pass the same flag and it restates a default in every one of them.
#
# configure reports:
#     --enable-static[=PKGS]  build static libraries [default=yes]
# where libgpg-error, libassuan, libksba, libgcrypt and npth all report
# [default=no]. Same flag, same spelling, opposite defaults -- and NOTHING BUT
# READING EACH configure --help TELLS YOU WHICH. Without it this package would
# ship a libseccomp.a alongside the shared library, which no other library recipe
# here does.
#
# It also retroactively justifies how the other six were documented. The reason
# recorded there was that "the default belongs to libtool rather than to the
# package, so a flag that restates a default today may not restate it
# permanently". This package is that argument made concrete: upstream overrode
# libtool's default in exactly the direction that would have caught anyone who
# assumed the flag was decorative.
#
# NOT PASSED: --enable-python. It is off by default and requires cython, which is
# not packaged -- so enabling it would be a hard configure error rather than a
# silent omission. Named because "the bindings would be useful" is a reasonable
# thought and the answer is that they cost a cython package first.
CONFIGURE_ARGS="--disable-static"
